  "_exec_summary": [
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000119913,
      "SummaryText": "The accidental release prevention and emergency response policies at the Shelton Terminal facility:\nNGL Supply Terminal Company is committed to the safety of its workers, the public, and the environment.  Its accidental release prevention program is an integrated approach, involving employee participation, management practices, written procedures, and training.  NGL has developed a comprehensive Liquefied Petroleum Gas (LPG) accident prevention program considering all aspects of process operation and maintenance.  The accident prevention program includes compliance with ongoing mechanical integrity inspections, which includes the API RP 510 Pressure Vessel Inspection Program and API RP 570 Piping Inspection Program.  In addition, the facility conducts 3 year PSM compliance audits.  If an emergency were to occur, it is NGL's policy to notify the South Mason Fire Department and request that they respond to the emergency. The Shelton Terminal also maintains incipient stage fire fighting equipment on site. Additionally, this facility complies with NFPA-58 requirements for LPG storage, and it is NGL's policy to adhere to all applicable federal, state, and local laws.  The facility came online in 2008. \n\nA description of the Shelton Terminal facility and the regulated substances handled: \nThis facility is a bulk terminal storing LPG for resale to retail propane dealers.  Product comes in via truck or railcars and is loaded into transport trucks and bobtails for delivery to customers.  The facility consists of four (4) 30,000 gallon storage tanks holding approximately 110,000 pounds each of liquid propane.  There is also the capacity for six (6) 34,000- gallon rail cars onsite.\n\nThere are both manual and automatic emergency shutdown systems on all LPG equipment at the site.\n\n\nThe general accidental release prevention program and chemical-specific prevention steps:\nThis facility complies with EPA's accident prevention rule, the OSHA PSM standard, and all applicable state an"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000119913,
      "SummaryText": "d local codes and regulations.  The LPG system is designed, installed, and maintained in accordance with NFPA-58 and state law.\n\nFive-year accident history: \nNGL Supply Terminal Company acquired the facility in December 2013.  There has not been an accident involving LPG that caused deaths, injuries, property or environmental damage, evacuations, or any shelter in place.  \n\nThe emergency response program:\nNGL Supply Terminal Company has developed an emergency response plan specific to this facility. In the event of an emergency involving the Shelton Terminal's LPG system, it is NGL's policy to notify the Shelton Fire Department and request that they respond to the emergency.  NGL has trained employees in incipient stage fire fighting.  The Shelton Terminal has coordinated the facility's response plans with the fire department and local emergency planning committee; the South Mason fire department has an open invitation to visit the Shelton Terminal for training and to inspect the LPG storage system.  \n\nPlanned changes to improve safety:\nNGL Supply Terminal Company, LLC will continually strive for safety improvement.  Periodic process safety inspections and Process Hazard Analyses will be conducted followed with timely response."
    }
